WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.680
If you go back and revisit some of the television
you watched as a kid, you might be surprised

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.380
about what networks used to regularly throw
in front of you.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.839
Some of our most beloved childhood favorites
and cult classics were actually pretty terrifying

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:12.959
in their own ways...

00:00:12.959 --> 00:00:16.849
Here are some TV shows that gave us nightmares
as kids.

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:18.789
Are You Afraid Of The Dark?

00:00:18.789 --> 00:00:22.449
Probably every kid's favorite scary show in
the '90s, Are You Afraid of the Dark?

00:00:22.449 --> 00:00:25.029
included quite a few nightmare-inducing moments.

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:28.820
For example, "The Tale of the Pinball Wizard,"
which kept probably kept some kids away from

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.680
arcades for a few years…

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:31.720
"Excuse me sir."

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.291
"Hey, Ah, Ah"

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.640
How about "The Tale of the Dark Music," which
proved that monsters living in closets are

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.000
pretty creepy — especially when they're
being polite?

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.800
"Hello Andy, come on in."

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:57.879
Even watching it today, there are some pretty
horrific concepts; alien abductors, the old

00:00:57.879 --> 00:01:02.270
lady down the road being a witch, ghost clowns
— these terrors and more could all be found

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.180
on cable TV, and during many sleepless nights.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.180
The Nightmare Room

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.080
"When the lights fade and the moon rises
anything can happen."

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.290
The WB's version of Are You Afraid of the
Dark?, The Nightmare Room also included a

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.250
splash of The Twilight Zone, and it was occasionally
just as scary as both.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:24.210
Based on the book series by R.L. Stine, some
of the episodes were truly disturbing.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.770
Like "Dear Diary, I'm Dead," for instance
— because nothing's better than reminding

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:32.040
children of their own impending mortality
and the fact that they may die at any moment!

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.380
Many of the episodes dealt with ghosts and
haunted things, but aliens and cursed summer

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.840
camps also figured in.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.610
Unfortunately, the show proved short-lived,
only lasting one season.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.610
Goosebumps

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:46.460
Also a product of the kid-warping mind of
R.L. Stine, the Goosebumps series of books

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:50.420
was too scary to read after dark, and the
TV show didn't disappoint either.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.780
Perhaps the creepiest part of the series was
Slappy, an evil ventriloquist dummy that was

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.700
basically a slightly less murder-y version
of Chucky.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.810
Slappy was created out of coffin wood by an
ancient sorcerer, and was hell-bent on enslaving

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:03.810
children...like you do.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.670
"You and I are one now, inseparable.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.649
You are my slave."

00:02:08.649 --> 00:02:10.580
Puppets are creepy enough as it is…

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.580
"Ahhh"

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.180
"I won't hurt you."

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:16.620
But we could never look at puppets the same
way after Slappy had his way with us

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.540
"Hey that dummy can't hurt you.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:20.500
He's not even alive.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.740
He's dead"

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.360
"Ahhhh"

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.720
"Ahhhh"

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.209
Eerie, Indiana

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:35.969
Another one-season show, Eerie, Indiana, was
like an X-Files for kids, with all the horror

00:02:35.969 --> 00:02:38.540
intact, and no bizarre alien pregnancies.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.879
Many of the episodes revolved around urban
legends like Bigfoot and other strange beings...with

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:47.109
the occasional gruesome death of a classmate,
resulting in a haunted heart transplant.

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:51.400
Because again, reminding kids that life is
fragile is probably the fastest way to ruin

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.060
the rest of their lives.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.060
Doctor Who

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.420
Today, the British science fiction series
Doctor Who enjoys plenty of critical acclaim

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.879
and mainstream fandom, but when the show first
aired in 1963, it was intended to be an educational

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:05.879
program.

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:09.249
By the time it hit its stride in the '70s
and '80s, it looked like the taxpayer-funded

00:03:09.249 --> 00:03:14.389
production that it was: low-budget, limited
sets, and guys in cheap suits portraying aliens

00:03:14.389 --> 00:03:15.389
and monsters.

00:03:15.389 --> 00:03:19.580
Still, the show's deadly serious tone, combined
with relentless rubber creatures who spoke

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.719
in unusual accents, hey, I take offence to
that, made the show fairly creepy for kids

00:03:23.719 --> 00:03:26.489
who snuck out of bed to see what their parents
were watching.

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:27.720
Courage the Cowardly Dog

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.359
Pit a timid, purple dog against a parade of
surreal monsters at a farmhouse in the middle

00:03:32.359 --> 00:03:34.799
of nowhere and you have Courage the Cowardly
Dog.

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:40.090
The Cartoon Network original series launched
in 1999 and had no shortage of supernatural

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:43.779
malevolence, from rooms made entirely of guts
to mind-control parasites.

00:03:43.779 --> 00:03:47.109
Oh, and the occasional end of all life on
Earth as well.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:50.170
Because the only thing better than reminding
kids that they're going to die is reminding

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:54.340
them that everyone else is going to die, too,
Yay.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:55.370
Slim Goodbody

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.169
This recurring series of short segments, which
ran during Captain Kangaroo in the '70s, was

00:04:00.169 --> 00:04:04.780
supposed to teach kids good habits, like why
it's important to exercise and that vegetables

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.780
are healthy.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.730
But creator and Slim Goodbody actor John Burstein
went about doing that in a questionable way:

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:15.560
by wearing a form-fitting, flesh-colored unitard
decorated with realistically rendered pictures

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.540
of the inner workings of the human body.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.900
Organs, bones, veins, blood, guts... everything.

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:24.600
Unfortunately, Slim Goodbody wound up looking
like a serial killer who glues his victim's

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.090
organs to his body.

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:29.300
Amazingly, Burstein still tours schools with
his Slim Goodbody character.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.200
More amazingly, schools let this guy in the
door.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.040
"I'm wonderful and special."

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.590
You know what else is wonderful and special?

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.220
Keeping your organs on the inside of your
body, you freak.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.340
"Ahhh"

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:44.400
Thanks for watching!

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.820
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.070
And leave us a comment to let us know which
shows freaked you out as a kid...

